# =============================================================================
# agent-kanban — deployment configuration
# Copy to .env and edit what you need:  cp .env.example .env
#
# This file is intentionally in English: it is read by whoever deploys the
# board, not only by the project maintainers.
# =============================================================================

# ---- Admin token (strongly recommended) ----
# Must be `k_` followed by 32 lowercase hex characters.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 16
#
# Why you should set it:
#   · The token survives container rebuilds (otherwise you dig it out of the
#     logs every single time).
#   · It can come straight from a CI variable or a K8s Secret, with no manual
#     copying.
#   · It is never written to config.toml nor printed in plaintext at startup.
#
# Leave it empty and the server generates one on first boot, prints it once,
# and stores it in /data/.kanban/config.toml.
# ⚠ .env holds a plaintext credential. Do not commit it (.env is gitignored).
KANBAN_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# ---- Port and exposure ----
# KANBAN_BIND is the **host-side** bind address:
#   127.0.0.1 = reachable from this machine only (default, safest)
#   0.0.0.0   = directly reachable from other machines on the LAN
# ⚠ This compose file ships no reverse proxy. TLS is the job of infrastructure
#   you already run (corporate gateway / nginx / Kubernetes Ingress).
#   Before switching to 0.0.0.0, confirm: ① something terminates TLS in front,
#   ② a firewall or security group restricts the source IPs.
#   Without TLS, tokens travel in plaintext.
# Note: the container itself always binds 0.0.0.0 (otherwise it would not
#       receive traffic from the Docker network). This variable alone decides
#       how far the server is reachable.
KANBAN_BIND=127.0.0.1
KANBAN_PORT=7788
